========================================================================================
[C] COMPLEXITY -- textbook cost, and the primitive that dominates it
========================================================================================
  n=|V|=1892  m=|E|=25434  K=20  C=100  h_sel=0.1
  t = mean |MIIA(v,h)|; each arborescence is a Dijkstra on -ln pp,
  costing O(t log t).  So (count, mean size) IS the empirical cost.

  CTIM
      cost : O(nC)  Eq (19)  +  sum_c O(n_c * t_c log t_c)  init  +  O(CK) DP cells
      why  : n arborescences, each on an INDUCED subgraph -> t_c is small
      wall clock: 0.47s

  CTIM-G
      cost : O(n * t log t)  global init  +  sum_c O(n_c * t log t)  curves  +  O(C K^2) DP  +  O(K * t^2) realisation
      why  : n arborescences, each on the FULL graph -> t is the global mean
      MEASURED: 2811 arborescences built, mean size 3.2
      MEASURED: 965 curve gain evals, 133 realisation gain evals
      MEASURED: 799 curve points built
      wall clock: 0.38s

  CTIM-G-lazy
      cost : O(n * t log t)  global init  +  O(K log C)  community heap  +  curve points built only on demand  +  O(K * t^2) realisation
      why  : same objective and split as CTIM-G; builds ~K curve points, not ~C*K
      MEASURED: 2162 arborescences built, mean size 3.9
      MEASURED: 208 curve gain evals, 133 realisation gain evals
      MEASURED: 20 curve points built
      wall clock: 0.38s

  full-graph vs induced-subgraph arborescence size
  (the ratio below is why CTIM is cheap and GlobalGreedy is not)
      sampled 200 nodes in 0.08s
      mean |MIIA| on the FULL graph      : 3.85
      mean |MIIA| on the INDUCED subgraph: 3.42
      ratio  full / induced             : 1.1x
      Both CTIM and GlobalGreedy build ~n arborescences; this ratio
      is the per-arborescence cost gap between them.

  item 86 done in 10.30s   (Eq (12) weights 2.39s, shared by every method)

